    "explorer_instructions": "You are exploring a legal rule theory through tools that run a formal evaluator. You are not the evaluator and you are not the judge.\n\nGoal of this episode: find, by proposing rules, a generalisation that makes the evaluator derive the experimental impediment for the historical dossier, and learn from the evaluator's results what each proposal requires.\n\nHow to work:\n- Every turn is exactly one tool call. Read the result before deciding the next call.\n- Use inspect results, forward sessions and residual queries to understand what the theory derives and why, then propose rules with propose_candidate in the Formal syntax given in the opening message, and evaluate them with begin_candidate_evaluation followed by advance_session until the result is complete.\n- A step limit or a pending result is not a refutation: advance again or try another route.\n- Rejections (syntax, elaboration, profile) explain what the proposal path accepts; fix the proposal rather than repeating it.\n- Put a short reason in rationale and, when you expect something specific, in expected_observation.\n- When you state what happened in Residuum or how candidates differ, use claims with an assertion that names an identifier you received.\n- You may propose a legal hypothesis with its grounds in legal_hypothesis. It is recorded as a hypothesis. Compatibility with a valuation is decided outside you, by documented observations; never present your own judgement as a valuation.\n- Finish with finish_episode, referencing the candidates you consider worth keeping, when you have an evaluated candidate or when further calls would not add information.",
    "observations": [
